.TH SUDO 1 "2026-11-08" "holyc" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sudo \- run a command as another user, a drop-in for sudo(8)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo
[\fB\-u\fR \fIUSER\fR] [\fB\-g\fR \fIGROUP\fR] [\fB\-EHnSb\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.br
.B sudo \-i
|
.B \-s
[\fICOMMAND\fR]
.br
.B sudo \-l
[\fICOMMAND\fR]
.br
.B sudo \-v
|
.B \-k
|
.B \-K
.br
.B sudo \-\-check
[\fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B sudo \-\-tui
.SH DESCRIPTION
Same command line, same
.I /etc/sudoers
language and the same PAM login as the original, without the plugin system, LDAP or I/O logging.
The rule grammar is complete for everyday use: user, runas and host lists, the four alias kinds,
.BR ! ,
.BR %group ,
.BR #uid ,
wildcards, directories, argument patterns, the tags NOPASSWD PASSWD SETENV NOEXEC, the last matching rule wins,
.B #include
and
.BR #includedir .
Defaults understood: timestamp_timeout, passwd_tries, env_reset, env_keep, secure_path, authenticate. Others are ignored.
.PP
Authentication goes through PAM service "sudo". A successful login is remembered per terminal for timestamp_timeout minutes
(files in /run/sudo/ts). The environment is reset except for a short list (TERM, DISPLAY, WAYLAND_DISPLAY, LANG, ...) and env_keep;
\fB\-E\fR needs the SETENV tag. Every run and every refusal is logged to syslog (authpriv).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u, \-g
target user and group (\fB#N\fR for numbers)
.TP
.B \-i, \-s
login shell, or shell of the target user (with a command: run it through the shell)
.TP
.B \-l
list what you may run; with a command, print its path if allowed (exit 1 otherwise)
.TP
.B \-v, \-k, \-K
refresh the timestamp, forget this terminal's, forget all of yours
.TP
.B \-n, \-S
never prompt (fail instead), read the password from stdin
.TP
.B \-\-check [FILE]
parse a sudoers file and report errors (like visudo \-c)
.TP
.B \-\-tui
interactive rule manager (see below)
.SH TUI
.B sudo \-\-tui
shows every rule that applies. Rules in the managed drop-in
.I /etc/sudoers.d/90-sudo-tui
can be added (\fBa\fR), deleted (\fBd\fR) and switched between asking for a password and NOPASSWD (\fBn\fR); \fBc\fR checks the whole
configuration; \fBj/k\fR or arrows move; \fBq\fR quits. Rules from other files are shown read-only. Each change is written to a temporary
file, verified with \fB\-\-check\fR and only then moved into place, so a typo cannot break sudo. Only users who may run everything can use it.
.SH INSTALLATION
.nf
cc -O2 -o sudo sudo.c -lpam
chown root:root sudo && chmod 4755 sudo
.fi
Needs /etc/pam.d/sudo (every distribution ships one) and a sudoers file owned by root and not writable by others.
Refuses with a clear error when it is not setuid root.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/sudoers, /etc/sudoers.d/*, /run/sudo/ts/*
.SH BUGS
Not done: sudoedit (\-e), per\-command Defaults, NOEXEC enforcement, \-\-askpass, session/PAM session modules, closing the
race of a command path changing between match and exec beyond what exec of the resolved path gives. Tested only unprivileged
(target = yourself); the setuid path with real root and PAM has not been exercised yet.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudoers (5),
.BR visudo (8),
.BR su (1)
